# API documentation

A screen is half of a frontend; the other half is the API it talks to. The documentation of every API this project integrates with is kept here, so that the agent
(and you) build against the operations that are **documented**, in the same way the screens are built from the design tokens instead of from guesses.

<%#hasBackend%>
This project has a backend of its own: its contract, [`.ai/contract/openapi.json`](../contract/openapi.json), is already its API documentation, and `api list` includes it as `backend`.
Import documentation here for any **other** API the screens call (a payment provider, a partner's service, a backend that lives in another repository).
<%/hasBackend%>
<%^hasBackend%>
This project has no backend of its own, so what its screens call is documented here. Without it, the only honest PRD API integration section is `No API calls.`.
<%/hasBackend%>

## Bringing the documentation in

```bash
./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc api import api.yaml                       # an OpenAPI 3.x or Swagger 2.0 document, JSON or YAML
./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc api import https://api.example.com/openapi.json   # the same, from a link (also: a documentation page; it looks for the document beside it)
./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc api import collection.json --name partner-api # a Postman collection (v2.x)
./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc api import api-guide.pdf --name partner-guide  # a guide written for people: .pdf, .docx, .md, .txt (its text is kept beside it)
```

Along with a requirements document, in one step:

```bash
./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc spec requirements.pdf --api api.yaml      # import the API documentation, then draft the spec from both
```

Each import creates `.ai/api/<name>/` with the document exactly as it is, and for an OpenAPI, Swagger or Postman document a generated `endpoints.md`: one line per operation with its
parameters, request body and documented responses. `.ai/api/apis.json` records every file with its size and checksum. Importing a name again replaces it and prints which operations were added or removed.

| What | How exact | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| **OpenAPI 3.x / Swagger 2.0** (JSON or YAML) | The operations, parameters, request and response types, servers and auth schemes are read from the document | A YAML document that uses anchors, aliases or tags is kept, but no index is built (convert it to JSON for the full benefit) |
| **Postman collection** | The requests (method, path, query) and the examples saved in it | A collection has examples, not types: `api show` prints the example bodies |
| **A link** | The same as a file; a link to a documentation page is followed to `/openapi.json`, `/swagger.json`, `/v3/api-docs` and a few other usual places | Credentials in the link are refused (they would be written down); download a protected document and import the file |
| **A guide** (PDF, Word, Markdown, text) | Stored as it is, with its text extracted | It has no machine-readable operations, so the PRD's operations are not checked against it |

## Reading it

```bash
./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc api list                       # every documented operation, grouped by tag
./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc api list --grep order          # only the lines that mention "order"
./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc api show GET /pets/{petId}     # one operation with its parameters, body, responses and every type it uses
```

## What is checked

- **`./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc api check`** (the `repo.api` gate<%^apiEnforced%>, switched off here by `api.enforce: false`<%/apiEnforced%>): every file in `apis.json` exists and is byte for byte what was imported, every generated `endpoints.md` is current, and
  nothing sits in `.ai/api/` that `apis.json` does not list. A document cannot be edited or swapped unnoticed.
<%#apiEnforced%>
- **The PRD's API integration section.** Each feature lists the operations it calls, written the way the documentation writes them (`` `GET /pets/{petId}` ``), or says `No API calls.`. The spec fails when it names an operation that no imported
  document (or this project's backend contract) has, and says what is closest. This is the check that stops an agent from calling an endpoint it made up.
<%/apiEnforced%>
- **Agents** are given the documents and the index (`implement` and `spec` both do), and may not edit `.ai/api/`: it is protected like the specs and the gate configuration.

## What it does not prove

That the real API behaves as it is documented, and that the documentation is current. The tests of a screen use responses shaped as the documentation says; whether the live service answers like that is for you to check
(import the document again when the API changes, and read what was added or removed). `./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc api import` the new version, review the diff, commit it.
